A||||13||Kasdan||Motor Swap begins....||||1||
Z||000000||Kasdan||10-15-2001||09:41 AM||||This morning my performance rebuilt 2.8 longblock was delivered to the place where my car is, and they will start the swap today.  They are charging me ~800 in labor, and they say the swap will be done wednesday afternoon.  is that too soon?  how long does it take to do a desent swap?  technically it can be done in a matter of hours if you have all needed parts available, but we know that never happens.<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">           <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||10-15-2001||09:51 AM||||Well, it all depends on what you are swapping from.  If you are just swapping a 2.8 engine with a 2.8 engine than I think $800 is too much.  <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||10-15-2001||11:16 AM||||Considering that folks get 7-800 for a clutch job, I don't think it's too terrible.<BR>Maybe because it's not <I>my</I> money.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Glad you're making progress, though.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||10-15-2001||12:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Considering that folks get 7-800 for a clutch job, I don't think it's too terrible.<BR>Maybe because it's not <I>my</I> money.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Glad you're making progress, though.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But what you are refering to as a clutch job is usually including the clutch, pressure plate, release bearing as well as labor. That can be several hundred dollars of parts. I think he is referring to just the labor to swap engines not including parts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||KissMySSFiero||10-15-2001||01:14 PM||||He is right.  that is way too much.  How many hours labor did they quote you?  I dont see what it would be more than 8-10.  that being on the higher side.  Its about 8 hours for a clutch in most fwd cars.   at 10hrs your looking at $600 in labor at $60 an hour.  most factory dealers charge $65-70/hr.  I would argue that if i were you.  i think theyre trying to get more because your young and because its a fiero.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||tomcat06085||10-15-2001||01:14 PM||||Guys....if anybody can do it faster than 15 or 16 hours at 50 bucks an hour they deserve the job and the 800.00<P>I think most shops would have it in the 'shop manual' that engine swaps are two full days.<P><P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Thanks for the help!||||reg||14
Z||000006||Alex4mula||10-15-2001||01:15 PM||||Well.. swaping the engine includes swaping all the intake, water pump, balancer, pulleys, pan, flywheel, etc. etc. so it is more labor than just changing a clutch. I think $800 is not bad at all.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red & White (:||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||10-15-2001||01:18 PM||||What is the labor rate for the shop doing the swap?  If the rate is $50/hr the hours work out to be a 16 man-hour job.  I think doing an engine swap in 16 hours is not an unreasonable amout of time.  Actually, I think that is pretty quick. There may be more than one person working on the swap.<P>When you are running a business, what you pay for the job goes to pay not only salary of the mechanics and benefits but business overhead (rent, heat electric etc.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||tomcat06085||10-15-2001||01:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>What is the labor rate for the shop doing the swap?  If the rate is $50/hr the hours work out to be a 16 man-hour job.  I think doing an engine swap in 16 hours is not an unreasonable amout of time.  Actually, I think that is pretty quick. There may be more than one person working on the swap.<P>When you are running a business, what you pay for the job goes to pay not only salary of the mechanics and benefits but business overhead (rent, heat electric etc.)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lar...you must have run a business somewhere. I agree...engine shops, job shops, service stations are paying their mechanics $20 to $25 an hour and ya have to pay for the building, equipment, utilities. If it was a buddy in his backyard or garage....800.00 might be a little steep, but not at a good shop ( trust is not a commodity like tires or oil changes ).<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Thanks for the help!||||reg||14
Z||000009||Archie||10-15-2001||08:35 PM||||Ditto what Oldcat and TomLar said.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000010||Kasdan||10-15-2001||09:31 PM||||Ok, turns out the shop is charging 55 an hour cuz i know the guy.  Hes starting the swap in the morning (tuesday)  because the block didnt get there until late.  He says itll be road worthy wednesday afternoon.||||reg||1
